From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mali_kbase: disable page migration on kernels without __SetPageMovable

Kernel 7.2 removed the per-page/per-mapping page-migration API
(__SetPageMovable()/__ClearPageMovable()) in favour of a per-pagetype
set_movable_ops(ops, pagetype) registry (mm/migrate.c). That registry
only has slots wired up for two in-tree users -- PGTY_offline (balloon)
and PGTY_zsmalloc -- with an explicit -EINVAL default for anything
else; there is no supported mechanism for an out-of-tree third-party
driver such as mali_kbase to register its own movable_operations.
Extending mm/migrate.c's switch (and the page_movable_ops() dispatcher,
and reserving a page_type bit) would be a core-MM change carried
out-of-tree indefinitely -- judged too high-risk to attempt without
upstream review.

Instead, page-migration support for kbase-owned pages is treated as
unavailable on kernels this new: version_compat_defs.h provides no-op
__SetPageMovable()/__ClearPageMovable() shims, and
kbase_mem_migrate_init() is changed to leave page_migration_static_key
permanently at its initial "off" state (regardless of the insmod
parameter or the large-pages default) so
kbase_is_page_migration_enabled() always returns false. Every
meaningful effect in mali_kbase_mem_migrate.c (alloc/free metadata,
isolate/migrate/putback callbacks, MMU PGD migration) is already gated
behind that accessor, so this mirrors the driver's own already-
supported "page migration explicitly disabled" runtime mode
(insmod kbase_page_migration_enabled=0), just made unconditional on
kernels lacking the underlying API. No page is ever marked movable
when the kernel has no isolate/migrate/putback path for it.

Fixes the mali_kbase.ko build against kernel 7.2.0-rc4-sky1-ncz.
Codex (gpt-5.5) adversarial-reviewed: APPROVE, no correctness
blockers found.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/arm/midgard/mali_kbase_mem_migrate.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/arm/midgard/mali_kbase_mem_migrate.c
@@ -682,6 +682,21 @@
 {
 	struct kbase_mem_migrate *mem_migrate = &kbdev->mem_migrate;
 
+#if MALI_KBASE_NO_LEGACY_PAGE_MOVABLE_API
+	/* This kernel has no __SetPageMovable()/__ClearPageMovable() (see
+	 * version_compat_defs.h) and no way for an out-of-tree driver to
+	 * register with the replacement set_movable_ops() registry. Page
+	 * migration support is therefore unavailable on this kernel,
+	 * regardless of the insmod parameter or the large-pages default --
+	 * leave page_migration_static_key at its initial "off" count so
+	 * kbase_is_page_migration_enabled() always returns false and none
+	 * of the isolate/migrate/putback machinery in this file is ever
+	 * exercised.
+	 */
+	if (kbase_page_migration_enabled > 0)
+		dev_info(kbdev->dev,
+			 "Page migration was requested at insmod but is unsupported on this kernel (no page-movable-ops API for out-of-tree drivers); staying disabled.");
+#else
 	/* Page migration support compiled in, either explicitly or
 	 * by default, so the default behaviour is to follow the choice
 	 * of large pages if not selected at insmod. Check insmod parameter
@@ -697,6 +712,7 @@
 		if (kbase_page_migration_enabled)
 			static_branch_inc(&page_migration_static_key);
 	}
+#endif /* MALI_KBASE_NO_LEGACY_PAGE_MOVABLE_API */
 
 	spin_lock_init(&mem_migrate->free_pages_lock);
 	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&mem_migrate->free_pages_list);
--- a/include/linux/version_compat_defs.h
+++ b/include/linux/version_compat_defs.h
@@ -760,6 +760,49 @@
 }
 #endif
 
+#if (KERNEL_VERSION(7, 2, 0) <= LINUX_VERSION_CODE)
+/*
+ * Kernel 7.2 removed the per-page/per-mapping page-migration API
+ * (__SetPageMovable() / __ClearPageMovable(), see the movable_ops /
+ * address_space_ops branches in mali_kbase_mem_migrate.c) in favour of a
+ * per-pagetype set_movable_ops(ops, pagetype) registry (mm/migrate.c).
+ * That registry only has slots wired up for two in-tree users --
+ * PGTY_offline (balloon) and PGTY_zsmalloc -- with an explicit -EINVAL
+ * default for anything else (see the switch in mm/migrate.c's
+ * set_movable_ops()); there is no supported mechanism for an out-of-tree
+ * third-party driver such as mali_kbase to register its own
+ * movable_operations. Extending mm/migrate.c's switch (and the
+ * page_movable_ops() dispatcher, and reserving a page_type bit) would be a
+ * core-MM change carried out-of-tree indefinitely -- high risk, outside
+ * this driver's scope, and not something to do without upstream review.
+ *
+ * Instead, page-migration support for kbase-owned pages is treated as
+ * unavailable on kernels this new: __SetPageMovable()/__ClearPageMovable()
+ * become no-ops. This is safe because every meaningful effect in
+ * mali_kbase_mem_migrate.c is already gated behind
+ * kbase_is_page_migration_enabled(), which kbase_mem_migrate_init() now
+ * forces permanently false whenever this shim is active -- so a page is
+ * never marked movable when the kernel has no way to isolate/migrate/
+ * putback it. This mirrors the driver's own already-supported "page
+ * migration explicitly disabled" runtime mode (insmod
+ * kbase_page_migration_enabled=0); it is not new/untested behaviour, just
+ * made unconditional on kernels lacking the underlying API.
+ */
+#define MALI_KBASE_NO_LEGACY_PAGE_MOVABLE_API 1
+#include <linux/migrate.h>
+static inline void __SetPageMovable(struct page *page, const struct movable_operations *mops)
+{
+	(void)page;
+	(void)mops;
+}
+static inline void __ClearPageMovable(struct page *page)
+{
+	(void)page;
+}
+#else
+#define MALI_KBASE_NO_LEGACY_PAGE_MOVABLE_API 0
+#endif /* (KERNEL_VERSION(7, 2, 0) <= LINUX_VERSION_CODE) */
+
 #ifndef DEVFREQ_GOV_SIMPLE_ONDEMAND
 #define DEVFREQ_GOV_SIMPLE_ONDEMAND "simple_ondemand"
 #endif
